"Every day a computer makes countless small decisions. Your phone unlocks only if the passcode is right. A game ends if you run out of lives. A message goes out if you tap Send and you are online. But Lessons 1 and 2 showed that a computer holds nothing except switches and numbers. Nobody inside is thinking. So how can a pile of switches decide anything? The secret is in how the switches are wired together.": "1ca7dfb0ea41",
"Every day a computer makes countless small decisions. Your phone unlocks only if the passcode is right. A game ends if you run out of lives. A message goes out if you tap Send and you are online. But Lessons 1 and 2 showed that a computer holds nothing except switches and numbers. Nobody inside is thinking. So how can a pile of switches decide anything? The secret is in how the switches are wired together. Start with a battery, a light, and two switches, A and B. Wire the switches in a row, one after the other. Now the current has to pass through both, so the light turns on only if A and B are both on. This arrangement is called an AND gate. Next, wire the two switches side by side, so that the current has two roads to choose from. Now the light turns on if A or B is on, or both. This is an OR gate. The third kind is the simplest and the strangest. It has only one input, and its output is always the opposite. If the input is on, the output is off. If the input is off, the output is on. It is called a NOT gate, and in the model it is a switch built like a seesaw. AND, OR, and NOT are the three basic logic gates. Each one takes in 0s and 1s and gives out a single 0 or 1. You can write down everything a gate does in a small chart called a truth table. It has one row for every possible set of inputs. Two inputs can be set in four ways, so the table for AND has four rows, and only the last row, 1 and 1, gives a 1. In a real computer, nobody flips the switches by hand. The gates are made of transistors, which are switches turned on and off by electricity. That is why the output of one gate can become the input of the next. The idea is older than computers. In 1854, a largely self-taught English mathematician named George Boole published a book known as The Laws of Thought. He showed that logic could be worked out like algebra, with symbols and rules. His system is now called Boolean algebra, and it is the basis for designing computer circuits. Boole died in 1864, long before anyone built an electronic computer. Decades later, a student named Claude Shannon showed that Boole’s algebra fits circuits made of switches. One gate answers one small question. The power comes from joining gates together. “Go out to play if it is NOT raining AND your homework is done” needs two gates: a NOT and an AND. Join more of them, and a circuit can compare two numbers, choose between two paths, or, as you will see in the next lesson, add. A modern chip holds billions of transistors wired into gates. Every decision a computer seems to make is really a great many tiny yes-or-no questions, answered by gates one after another.": "ffa9d989eee9",
